/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  postDict -- SIZING and COSTING of every unit that is a piece of equipment
  (DEV.md 4c C44, slice 1), of the PRODUCT STORAGE (slice 2), of what each
  vessel HOLDS while the plant runs (slice 3), and the ECONOMIC APPRAISAL
  with its WORKING CAPITAL built up from those holdings (slice 4).

  INVENTORY (what each item holds, "retida em cada equipamento").  An item
  may declare `inventory { held ( { stream <s>; fraction <f>; } ... ); }`:
  it then holds, per phase, V x fraction x rho x w, where V is its own
  sized volume and rho and w the density and mass fractions of the stream
  named for that phase, at the state that stream carries.  The phase is
  READ off the stream, never declared.  Declared here, every fraction an
  AUTHOR-SET ASSUMPTION:
    Converter     the gas in the bed voids: 0.40 of the catalyst volume
                  (a typical packed-bed voidage, not measured), at the
                  converter outlet state (HotEffluent); the rest is catalyst.
    Separator     a liquid level of 0.25 (RawLiquid) under 0.75 of gas
                  (UnreactedGas).
    LetdownHP     half liquid (LetdownLiquid), half gas (LetdownGas).
    StorageFlash  half liquid (ProductNH3), half gas (FlashGas).
  The two LET-DOWN drums' volumes are lower bounds (see below), so their
  inventories are too.  The storage tanks hold their own working level --
  21 days of production -- and declare nothing more.  NOT DECLARED, and the
  run lists them by name rather than as zero: the compressors and the
  exchangers.  They hold gas, but no sizer here publishes the volume they
  hold it in, and none is invented.  For scale only: the whole 25 m3
  Separator holds ~0.9 t of loop gas, against the tanks' ~25 000 t.

  WHAT IS SIZED, AND ON WHAT BASIS.  Fourteen of the seventeen units:

    compressors (4)   N2Boost MakeupComp1 MakeupComp2 RecycleComp
                      by SHAFT POWER, the unit's own result (pass-through).
    exchangers  (6)   N2Cooler Intercooler Aftercooler WaterCooler Chiller
                      FEHE -- DESIGNED in detail by the Kern method
                      (`design {}` below; DEV.md 4c C48), no longer
                      A = Q/(U*LMTD) on a typed U.  The AUTHOR chooses the
                      bundle and the fouling; the RUN finds the smallest
                      number of tubes N for which
                          U * F * LMTD * n_shells * pi d_o L N >= |Q|,
                      U on the outside area from the two films (Gnielinski
                      in the tubes, Kern on the shell), the tube wall (the
                      item's material record's conductivity) and the
                      declared fouling; F of one 1-2 shell (Bowman, Mueller
                      & Nagle 1940), or 1 for one tube pass or a boiling
                      utility.  The SAME kernel the two-stream heatExchanger
                      unit rates and designs with (htc/ShellTubeDesign).
                      The sheet carries N, the shell ID, the baffles, h on
                      each side, the five resistances whose sum is 1/U, U
                      clean and dirty, F, the area required and installed,
                      the utility flow and both pressure drops.  The
                      INSTALLED area is what is costed.
                      Properties: the process gas by the case's own package
                      (constant/thermoPhysPropDict, whose `transport {}`
                      exists for this) at the MEAN of its terminal
                      temperatures and its inlet pressure; the cooling water
                      by the system its OWN record declares (IF97,
                      data/standards/utilities/coolingWater.dat), its flow
                      |Q| / dutyPerKg.  The LMTD is still COMPUTED from the
                      unit's own temperatures and the utility's -- no
                      temperature is typed: every cooler inlet here is a
                      compressor discharge, which moves the day a group
                      re-tunes a shaft power.
                      THE CHOICES, all AUTHOR-SET, none cited:
                        * tubes 25.4 mm OD x 2.77 mm wall (19.86 mm ID),
                          6.096 m (20 ft), pitch 31.75 mm (1.25 OD) -- the
                          gas in the TUBES, because it is the high-pressure
                          side; SQUARE pitch where the cooling water is on
                          the shell (cleanable lanes), triangular elsewhere;
                        * 2 tube passes on the water coolers; 4 on the
                          Chiller (its utility boils, so F = 1 whatever the
                          passes and passes only buy gas velocity); ONE on
                          the FEHE -- its ends cross (hot out 336 K, cold out
                          651 K), so no 1-2 shell can serve it -- over FOUR
                          6.1 m shells in counter-current series;
                        * baffles at 1.0 x the shell ID, the widest
                          customary spacing: at 0.4 x the shell ID (the first
                          trial, measured) the water side lost 1.2-4.7 bar;
                        * fouling 0.0002 m2.K/W on the process gas, 0.00035
                          on the tower water, 0.0002 on the refrigerant --
                          TEMA-typical orders of magnitude; no table was read;
                        * the Chiller's BOILING-ammonia coefficient, 2500
                          W/m2/K, is TYPED (`utilitySideFilm`): a boiling
                          film is not a single-phase correlation and none is
                          wired into the design, so it is declared, said on
                          the sheet, and no shell-side pressure drop is
                          computed for it.
                      THREE THINGS THE SHEETS SAY, read them:
                        * the gas viscosity and conductivity are DILUTE-GAS
                          values (Chung, Eucken); at 150 bar the real gas is
                          more viscous and more conductive;
                        * the WaterCooler and the Chiller CONDENSE ammonia:
                          they are designed on the GAS film of the vapour
                          that leaves (its own equilibrium vapour, its own
                          mass flow), the condensate film -- which is better
                          -- not credited, so they are on the SAFE side; their
                          pressure drop is the whole inlet flow as gas;
                        * the FEHE's own 2000 m2 (U 550, declared on the
                          unit, which forms the loop's answer) is published
                          beside the design as A_rated / U_rated and moves
                          nothing; the DESIGNED area is costed, because it is
                          the hardware that delivers the same duty on
                          computed films and declared fouling.
                      Several exchangers exceed the cost correlation's
                      1000 m2 ceiling (a real plant splits such a duty over
                      several shells), and the costing pass says so.
    converter   (1)   a catalyst VESSEL on a DECLARED space velocity.  The
                      Gibbs reactor has no kinetics, so it has no volume of
                      its own; V = Q_normal(inlet) / SV is a design BASIS,
                      not a result.  The 20000 1/h (Nm3 gas / (m3 catalyst
                      h), normal basis, on the INLET gas) is the same
                      ASSUMED feasibility figure ammonia02 uses -- not a
                      datum for any catalyst.  A rate law sizes the beds
                      (ammoniaReactor04_kineticSizing); that is the groups' task 2.
    drums       (3)   Separator LetdownHP StorageFlash, by VAPOUR residence
                      time (V = Q_vapour * tau, ideal gas), the precedent
                      ammonia02 sets.  READ THIS: the Separator is a
                      knock-out drum and the basis fits it; the two LET-DOWN
                      drums are LIQUID drums -- 99.6 % and 98.6 % of the moles
                      entering them leave as liquid -- and their size is set
                      by liquid holdup, which this basis does not contain.
                      A liquid-holdup basis needs a liquid density, and the
                      sizing pass has no route to one today; their volumes
                      below are therefore LOWER BOUNDS (LetdownHP comes out
                      below the vessel correlation's range, and the costing
                      pass says so).  Named, not fixed: it is the
                      per-equipment inventory slice's question too.

    storage     (1)   NH3Storage, attached to the boundary stream ProductNH3
                      (`storage ( ... )` below; Vitor ruled a tank a postDict
                      item attached to a stream, DEV.md 4c C44).  Basis, all
                      AUTHOR-SET design assumptions, none cited:
                        * 21 days of production -- the commander's decision
                          for this case, a design basis, not a datum;
                        * refrigerated atmospheric storage at the
                          StorageFlash outlet (238.15 K, 1 atm), the
                          state the stream carries;
                        * a working level of 90 % of the tank volume;
                        * at most 30 000 m3 per tank -- the ceiling of the
                          tank cost correlation, so every tank is priced
                          inside its range.
                      V = 21 day x mdot / (rho x 0.90), split into equal tanks.
                      rho is the case's own liquid density at 238.15 K
                      (announced on the run; READ the README: the NH3
                      record's Vliq gives ~769 kg/m3 there, which is likely
                      ~12 % high, so the tanks are likely ~12 % small).
                      COST: the only tank row read (CAPCOST 2017, the
                      authors' program for Turton App. A) is an ATMOSPHERIC
                      API fixed-roof tank.  A refrigerated, insulated,
                      double-containment ammonia tank costs more, so the
                      storage cost below is a LOWER BOUND, said on every run.
                      A gas is refused by this tank sizer by name: a gas is
                      stored in pressure vessels -- `gasStorage ( ... )`,
                      the H2Buffer (GAS STORAGE, after the working capital
                      below).  NOT STORED HERE, by decision: the NITROGEN
                      supply belongs to the ASU, outside this battery limit,
                      and so does the electrolyser that makes the hydrogen;
                      the hydrogen BUFFER is in this battery limit.

  WHAT IS NOT SIZED, and why it is not an omission: LoopMix, MakeupMix and
  PurgeSplit are a tee and two pipe junctions -- no vessel, no area, no
  driver -- so they are not costed equipment and no item is invented for
  them.  Outside the battery limit, as the flowsheet header says: the
  electrolysers, the ASU, the cooling-water system, the refrigeration
  package and the steam system.

  EVERY DESIGN NUMBER BELOW IS AN AUTHOR-SET DESIGN ASSUMPTION, declared
  with its reason on its line, and none cites a source because none was
  read.  The rules they follow:
    * pressureDesign (GAUGE) = max(1.1 x Pg, Pg + 1.7 bar), the Design Guide's
      preliminary rule on the GAUGE operating pressure, rounded UP (not 1.1 x
      the absolute, as before C150 R01).  The loop shares one: 151.27 bar abs
      = 150.26 bar g -> 166 bar g.  For a compressor the value is recorded only: the
      correlation folds the discharge pressure into the power (F_P = 1).
    * MATERIAL, one rule: SS316 where the process side exceeds the carbon
      steel record's maxT (700 K) -- the converter outlet is at 733 K;
      SS304 where it goes below 0 C, because austenitic stainless keeps its
      toughness there and the generic carbon-steel record states no
      minimum design temperature; carbon steel everywhere else, HYDROGEN
      SERVICE INCLUDED.  The material records carry no hydrogen-service
      limit, so whether carbon steel resists hydrogen at these partial
      pressures and temperatures is NOT checked here -- it is the groups'
      materials question, and moving an item to SS316 is one word.  Every
      item designed above 100 bar also exceeds the material records' maxP
      (100 bar), and the run warns about every one of them -- the two
      vessels (Converter, Separator), the exchangers and the compressors
      (the exchangers and compressors since DEV.md 4c C86); the item is
      still sized and priced.  For a compressor
      the material is recorded only (the correlation's F_BM 2.7 carries no
      material factor).
    * U is no longer declared: it is each exchanger's design RESULT (the
      exchanger paragraph above lists what IS declared instead).
    * vessels: a fully radiographed joint (jointEfficiency 1.0) and 3 mm of
      corrosion allowance; L/D 6 for the catalyst vessel (ammonia02's), 3
      for the drums.

  The price constants are ammonia02's (CEPCI 2026 = 820 is a projection,
  not a published index value; EUR/USD 0.92).

  UTILITIES, C_UT (DEV.md 4c C52).  C_UT is the utility allocation priced:
  every heat duty a unit publishes is served by the lowest-grade utility in
  constant/utilities/ that can take it across dTmin 10 K, carried by the
  unit's own process streams (the FEHE), or LISTED unserved -- never dropped.
  Until C52 the converter's ~30.6 MW was dropped (the allocation read no
  reactor's duty).  It is now served by a DECLARED utility,
  `utility steamGenerationHP;` on the Converter: HP steam raised in a
  waste-heat boiler at the steamHP header's 41 bar, valued at -18 EUR/GJ --
  a CREDIT, an AUTHOR-SET ASSUMPTION stated in the case-local record
  constant/utilities/steamGenerationHP.dat (an upper bound: the steam is
  worth that only if the plant uses it).  The credit is printed on its own
  line under C_UT and published as C_UT_credit.  One duty stays UNSERVED:
  the StorageFlash cools to -35 C and the coldest utility boils at -33.3 C,
  so C_UT is a LOWER BOUND and says so.

  ECONOMICS (slice 4).  Prices are in constant/economics, every one an
  AUTHOR-SET assumption (its header says so).  The appraisal knobs below
  are ammonia02's, also assumptions.  N_np, the number of processing steps
  Turton's labour correlation counts (compressors, exchangers, reactors;
  not drums, tanks or pumps), is COUNTED from this flowsheet: 4 + 6 + 1 =
  11.

  WORKING CAPITAL, BUILT UP instead of 15 % of FCI.  The working capital is
  the money a running plant keeps tied up in things that are not equipment.
  Its GROSS figure -- the current assets, "capital circulante" -- is:
    raw-material stock    the HYDROGEN the full H2Buffer holds, at its
                          purchase price (a store attached to a priced raw
                          material is that raw material's stock: DEV.md
                          C89); and 0 days of nitrogen, which arrives by pipe
                          from the ASU, its buffer outside this battery
                          limit (the commander's decision, DEV.md C44);
    process inventory     what the vessels hold (slice 3), at cost;
    product stock         what the two product tanks hold -- 21 days of
                          production -- at the cost of making it, never at
                          its sales price;
    receivables           30 days of sales;
    minimum cash          15 days of the cash cost of manufacture (COM_d).
  Its NET figure -- "fundo de maneio" -- is the gross minus what the plant
  owes its suppliers (payables, 30 days of raw-material purchases).  The net
  is what the owners put in at year 0 and recover at the end, and it is the
  WC of the cash flow.  The run prints both, line by line, beside the 15 %
  figure for comparison.  The days are the commander's decisions for this
  case (DEV.md C44), design-basis assumptions, not data.

  GAS STORAGE (DEV.md 4c C89).
    H2 buffer         H2Buffer, attached to the boundary stream H2Feed
                      (`gasStorage ( ... )` below; DEV.md 4c C89).  WHY: the
                      electrolysers follow an intermittent renewable supply
                      and the synthesis loop wants a steady feed, so the
                      hydrogen is BUFFERED.  The vessels give back only the
                      gas between a full and an emptied state:
                          working gas  = autonomy x mdot(H2Feed)
                          V_total      = working gas
                                         / (rho(Pmax, T) - rho(Pmin, T))
                      rho from the case's OWN package (SRK; the run prints
                      Z at both ends -- hydrogen is supercritical here and
                      SRK puts it slightly above ideal), split into equal
                      STANDING vessels of at most maxVesselVolume (the Turton
                      vessel set the tree carries is the vertical one), each
                      wall at Pmax as gauge.
                      Basis, all AUTHOR-SET design assumptions, none cited:
                        * 2 h of autonomy at full rate -- a short-term buffer
                          that rides through a cloud or a wind lull; a
                          day/night or seasonal buffer is a different store
                          (caverns, pipelines) and the groups' question;
                        * Pmax 100 bar -- the carbonSteel record's own maxP
                          ("typical pressure-vessel rating"), so the store
                          sits inside the rating the record declares;
                        * Pmin 32 bar -- the make-up train takes H2 at 30
                          bar (MakeupMix), plus 2 bar for the let-down valve
                          that controls the delivery;
                        * 313.15 K, the stream's own temperature (the vessels
                          are uninsulated and take what they are given);
                        * at most 400 m3 per vessel, inside the vessel cost
                          correlation's 0.3-520 m3 range; L/D 3, 3 mm of
                          corrosion allowance and a fully radiographed joint,
                          the drums' own constants;
                        * COST BY STEEL MASS (Vitor's ruling on C89): each
                          vessel = its published shell steel (rho pi D H t,
                          the cylindrical shell only) x 8 EUR/kg, an
                          INSTALLED price per kg of shell with heads,
                          nozzles and supports folded in -- a round
                          placeholder, not a quotation and not from any
                          source.  No index and no module factor are
                          applied to it; like every item it carries the
                          1.18 contingency and fee (C_TM = 1.18 C_BM).  The Turton vessel set is printed
                          beside it as an EXTRAPOLATED comparison (F_P ~ 32
                          on a 200 mm wall) and is NOT in the total.
                      NOT INCLUDED, said on every run: the CHARGING
                      COMPRESSOR from 30 bar to Pmax (neither the machine nor
                      its power is sized or priced: the buffer's cost is a
                      LOWER BOUND), and any renewable-power profile -- the 2 h
                      are assumed, not derived.  The hydrogen the full
                      buffer holds is RAW-MATERIAL STOCK in the working
                      capital, at its purchase price.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
sizing
{
    units
    (
        //  ---- compressors: the shaft power is the unit's own result ------
        { unitName N2Boost;      type compressor;  material carbonSteel;      // dry N2, 474 K discharge
          designRules { pressureDesign 33; } }                                 // 30.2 bar abs = 29.19 bar g; max(1.1 x, +1.7 bar) -> 33 bar g
        { unitName MakeupComp1;  type compressor;  material carbonSteel;      // H2/N2, 420 K discharge
          designRules { pressureDesign 73; } }                                 // 66.7 bar abs = 65.69 bar g; max(1.1 x, +1.7 bar) -> 73 bar g
        { unitName MakeupComp2;  type compressor;  material carbonSteel;      // H2/N2, 425 K discharge
          designRules { pressureDesign 166; } }                                // the loop's
        { unitName RecycleComp;  type compressor;  material SS304;            // suction at -20 C
          designRules { pressureDesign 166; } }                                // the loop's

        //  ---- the six exchangers: DESIGNED in detail (Kern), see header ----
        //  Every geometry and fouling number below is AUTHOR-SET (see the
        //  header for each one's reason); U, N, the shell, the films, F, the
        //  area and both pressure drops are the design's RESULTS.
        { unitName N2Cooler;     type shellTubeHX;  material carbonSteel;
          designRules { utility coolingWater;      // 25 -> 35 C, the plant tower
                        pressureDesign 33;
                        design { method Kern;
                                 processSide tube;            // the 30 bar gas in the tubes
                                 tubeOD 25.4 mm;  tubeID 19.86 mm;   // 1 in, 2.77 mm wall
                                 tubeLength 6.096 m;          // 20 ft
                                 tubePitch 31.75 mm;  tubePattern square;  // 1.25 OD, cleanable water lanes
                                 tubePasses 2;
                                 baffleSpacingRatio 1.0;      // B = 1.0 x shell ID: see the header
                                 tubeSide  { model Gnielinski; }
                                 shellSide { model Kern; }
                                 fouling { tubeSide  0.0002  m2.K/W;    // clean process gas
                                           shellSide 0.00035 m2.K/W; }  // treated tower water
                               } } }
        { unitName Intercooler;  type shellTubeHX;  material carbonSteel;
          designRules { utility coolingWater;
                        pressureDesign 73;
                        design { method Kern;  processSide tube;
                                 tubeOD 25.4 mm;  tubeID 19.86 mm;  tubeLength 6.096 m;
                                 tubePitch 31.75 mm;  tubePattern square;  tubePasses 2;
                                 baffleSpacingRatio 1.0;
                                 tubeSide  { model Gnielinski; }
                                 shellSide { model Kern; }
                                 fouling { tubeSide 0.0002 m2.K/W;  shellSide 0.00035 m2.K/W; }
                               } } }
        { unitName Aftercooler;  type shellTubeHX;  material carbonSteel;
          designRules { utility coolingWater;
                        pressureDesign 166;
                        design { method Kern;  processSide tube;
                                 tubeOD 25.4 mm;  tubeID 19.86 mm;  tubeLength 6.096 m;
                                 tubePitch 31.75 mm;  tubePattern square;  tubePasses 2;
                                 baffleSpacingRatio 1.0;
                                 tubeSide  { model Gnielinski; }
                                 shellSide { model Kern; }
                                 fouling { tubeSide 0.0002 m2.K/W;  shellSide 0.00035 m2.K/W; }
                               } } }
        { unitName WaterCooler;  type shellTubeHX;  material carbonSteel;
          designRules { utility coolingWater;      // part of the NH3 condenses: gas-film basis
                        pressureDesign 166;
                        design { method Kern;  processSide tube;
                                 tubeOD 25.4 mm;  tubeID 19.86 mm;  tubeLength 6.096 m;
                                 tubePitch 31.75 mm;  tubePattern square;  tubePasses 2;
                                 baffleSpacingRatio 1.0;
                                 tubeSide  { model Gnielinski; }
                                 shellSide { model Kern; }
                                 fouling { tubeSide 0.0002 m2.K/W;  shellSide 0.00035 m2.K/W; }
                               } } }
        { unitName Chiller;      type shellTubeHX;  material SS304;            // process side to -20 C
          designRules { utility refrigerationNH3;  // -33.3 C, ammonia boiling at 1 atm
                        pressureDesign 166;
                        design { method Kern;  processSide tube;   // a kettle: gas in the tubes
                                 tubeOD 25.4 mm;  tubeID 19.86 mm;  tubeLength 6.096 m;
                                 tubePitch 31.75 mm;  tubePattern triangular;  // clean refrigerant
                                 tubePasses 4;                // F = 1 here: passes buy gas velocity
                                 baffleSpacingRatio 1.0;      // tube supports only: the film is declared
                                 tubeSide  { model Gnielinski; }
                                 utilitySideFilm 2500 W/m2/K; // boiling NH3 -- AUTHOR-SET, see header
                                 fouling { tubeSide 0.0002 m2.K/W;  shellSide 0.0002 m2.K/W; }
                               } } }
        { unitName FEHE;         type shellTubeHX;  material SS316;            // hot side 733 K
          designRules { pressureDesign 166;
                        design { method Kern;
                                 tubeStream HotEffluent;      // the hot effluent in the tubes
                                 tubeOD 25.4 mm;  tubeID 19.86 mm;  tubeLength 6.096 m;
                                 tubePitch 31.75 mm;  tubePattern triangular;
                                 tubePasses 1;                // counter-current: the ends cross
                                 shellsInSeries 4;            // 4 x 6.1 m shells in series
                                 baffleSpacingRatio 1.0;
                                 tubeSide  { model Gnielinski; }
                                 shellSide { model Kern; }
                                 fouling { tubeSide 0.0002 m2.K/W;  shellSide 0.0002 m2.K/W; }
                               } } }

        //  ---- the converter: a catalyst vessel on a declared basis ------
        { unitName Converter;    type vessel;  material SS316;                 // 733 K outlet
          designRules { spaceVelocity 20000;        // 1/h, Nm3 gas / (m3 catalyst h) -- ASSUMED
                        spaceVelocityBasis normal;  // the basis the number is quoted on
                        flowKey N_in_mol_s;         // GHSV is on the INLET gas
                        L_over_D 6;  pressureDesign 166;
                        corrosionAllow 0.003;  jointEfficiency 1.0; }
          inventory { held ( { stream HotEffluent;  fraction 0.40; } ); } }
                                                  // bed voidage -- ASSUMED;
                                                  // the rest is catalyst

        //  ---- the drums: vapour residence (see the header) --------------
        { unitName Separator;    type vessel;  material SS304;                 // -20 C
          designRules { residenceTime 45;           // s of vapour, a knock-out drum
                        flowKey F_beta;  flowUnit kmol/s;
                        L_over_D 3;  pressureDesign 166;
                        corrosionAllow 0.003;  jointEfficiency 1.0; }
          inventory { held ( { stream RawLiquid;     fraction 0.25; }     // liquid level -- ASSUMED
                             { stream UnreactedGas;  fraction 0.75; } ); } }
        { unitName LetdownHP;    type vessel;  material SS304;                 // -20 C
          designRules { residenceTime 60;           // s of vapour -- a LOWER BOUND, see header
                        flowKey F_beta;  flowUnit kmol/s;
                        L_over_D 3;  pressureDesign 21;                        // 20 bar abs = 18.99 bar g; max(1.1 x, +1.7 bar) -> 21 bar g
                        corrosionAllow 0.003;  jointEfficiency 1.0; }
          inventory { held ( { stream LetdownLiquid; fraction 0.50; }     // liquid level -- ASSUMED
                             { stream LetdownGas;    fraction 0.50; } ); } }
        { unitName StorageFlash; type vessel;  material SS304;                 // -35 C
          designRules { residenceTime 60;           // s of vapour -- a LOWER BOUND, see header
                        flowKey F_beta;  flowUnit kmol/s;
                        L_over_D 3;  pressureDesign 2;                         // 1.013 bar abs = 0 bar g; the +1.7 bar floor -> 2 bar g
                        corrosionAllow 0.003;  jointEfficiency 1.0; }
          inventory { held ( { stream ProductNH3;    fraction 0.50; }     // liquid level -- ASSUMED
                             { stream FlashGas;      fraction 0.50; } ); } }
    );

    //  ---- storage: attached to a boundary stream (see the header) ----
    storage
    (
        { name          NH3Storage;
          stream        ProductNH3;      // the product, at 238.15 K and 1 atm
          material      SS304;           // below 0 C (the rule above); the tank
                                         // row carries NO material factor
          storageTime   21 day;          // days of production -- AUTHOR-SET
          fillFraction  0.90;            // working level / tank volume -- AUTHOR-SET
          maxTankVolume 30000;           // m3 per tank: the correlation's ceiling
        }
    );

    //  ---- gas storage: the H2 buffer, pressure vessels (see the header) --
    gasStorage
    (
        { name            H2Buffer;
          stream          H2Feed;        // electrolytic H2, 30 bar, 313.15 K
          material        carbonSteel;   // hydrogen service NOT checked (the rule above)
          autonomy        2 h;           // hours at full rate -- AUTHOR-SET
          reason          "a short-term buffer that rides through a cloud or a wind lull of up to two hours at full rate; a day/night or seasonal buffer is a different store (caverns, pipelines) and the groups' question";
          maxPressure     100 bar;       // absolute: the carbonSteel record's maxP -- AUTHOR-SET
          minPressure     32 bar;        // absolute: the 30 bar make-up suction + 2 bar valve -- AUTHOR-SET
          temperature     313.15 K;      // the stream's own -- AUTHOR-SET
          maxVesselVolume 400;           // m3 per vessel, inside the cost correlation's range
          L_over_D 3;  corrosionAllow 0.003;  jointEfficiency 1.0;   // the drums' constants
          steelPrice      8;             // EUR per kg of SHELL steel, INSTALLED -- AUTHOR-SET
          steelPriceReason "a round placeholder for a fabricated, installed carbon-steel pressure vessel per kg of its shell, heads, nozzles and supports folded in; not a quotation and not taken from any source -- replace it with a vendor's price";
        }
    );
}
costing
{
    method    Turton;
    year      2026;
    cepci     820;        // projection, author-set (see the header)
    cepci2001 397;        // the correlation's own basis year
    usdToEur  0.92;
}
economics
{
    method              discountedCashFlow;
    projectLife         15;          // yr -- assumption (ammonia02's)
    constructionPeriod  1;
    discountRate        0.10;        // assumption
    taxRate             0.21;        // assumption
    depreciation        straightLine;
    depreciableLife     9;           // yr
    salvageFraction     0.0;
    streamFactor        0.90;        // 7884 h/yr on stream -- assumption
    siteFactor          0.50;
    contingencyFee      1.18;
    N_np                11;          // 4 compressors + 6 exchangers + 1 reactor
    estimateClass       4;           // the AUTHOR's statement of how far the project is defined (AACE's primary characteristic) -- not a consequence of the model or of the design stage illustrated
    accuracyBand        ( -30 50 );  // the wide corner of Class 4 -- chosen
    refuseOnMissingPrice 1;

    //  THE WORKING CAPITAL, built up (see the header).  Every key needs its
    //  unit: a bare number would be read as SECONDS.
    workingCapital
    {
        rawMaterialStock   0 day;    // H2 and N2 by pipe; no stock here
        receivables       30 day;    // days of sales customers owe
        payables          30 day;    // days of raw-material purchases owed
        minimumCash       15 day;    // days of cash operating cost (COM_d)
    }

    //  THE LAND (DEV.md 4c C82): the plot area the layout publishes
